<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>More details on Algorithm 1</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 LogSTOP: An Algorithm for Computing STOPs ‣ LogSTOP: Temporal Scores over Prediction Sequences for Matching and Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> describes how LogSTOP is computed for a sequence <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X[t_{s}:t_{e}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X[t_{s}:t_{e}]</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to temporal property <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>, given start and end timesteps <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>, and smoothing window <math id="A1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. Here we discuss the intuition behind the operators, along with some examples:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Downsampling and average smoothing of confidence scores of local properties.</span> Firstly, LogSTOP downsamples the sequence of length <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to contiguous blocks of length <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. The confidence scores for any local property in each block starting at <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}(c,t^{\prime}\in[t,t+w])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}(c,t^{\prime}\in[t,t+w])</annotation></semantics></math> are first averaged after being normalized to the <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> range. The confidence score for each block is then the <math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>log</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log</annotation></semantics></math> of this averaged <math id="A1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>-normalized score. This series of downsampling, normalizing and smoothing operations starting at timestep <math id="A1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> for a window <math id="A1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> can be seen on line <a href="#alg1.l8" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 LogSTOP: An Algorithm for Computing STOPs ‣ LogSTOP: Temporal Scores over Prediction Sequences for Matching and Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. For example, given a sequence of log scores for object "car",</p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}({car},t\in[0,5])=[\log(0.9),\log(0.1),\log(0.9),\log(0.9),\log(0.9),\log(0.9)]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}({car},t\in[0,5])=[\log(0.9),\log(0.1),\log(0.9),\log(0.9),\log(0.9),\log(0.9)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="A1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=3</annotation></semantics></math>, the LogSTOP first downsamples to 2 blocks,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[[\log(0.9),\log(0.1),\log(0.9)],[\log(0.9),\log(0.9),\log(0.9)]]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[[\log(0.9),\log(0.1),\log(0.9)],[\log(0.9),\log(0.9),\log(0.9)]]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">before averaging by block,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\log((0.9+0.1+0.9)/3),\log((0.9+0.9+0.9)/3)]=[\log(0.63),\log(0.9)]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>0.9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>0.9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.63</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\log((0.9+0.1+0.9)/3),\log((0.9+0.9+0.9)/3)]=[\log(0.63),\log(0.9)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">In this example, note that the score <math id="A1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(0.1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(0.1)</annotation></semantics></math> is likely incorrect since the car cannot momentarily disappear. Downsampling and then smoothing reduce the impact of this incorrect local prediction hence essentially capturing the property that confidence scores cannot drastically change in a local window.
Note that this is done online for each successive block and the shifting for temporal operators is handled by the Next operator (line <a href="#alg1.l16" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 LogSTOP: An Algorithm for Computing STOPs ‣ LogSTOP: Temporal Scores over Prediction Sequences for Matching and Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Handling of logical operators (<math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neg,\land,\lor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∧</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∨</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neg,\land,\lor</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>.
The LogSTOP for <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neg\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neg\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> at timestep <math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is intuitively high when the score for <math id="A1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is low (line <a href="#alg1.l10" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 LogSTOP: An Algorithm for Computing STOPs ‣ LogSTOP: Temporal Scores over Prediction Sequences for Matching and Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). For example, given a high score <math id="A1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}({car},t,w)=\log(0.9)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}({car},t,w)=\log(0.9)</annotation></semantics></math>, the score for "not car" <math id="A1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}(\neg{car},t,w)=\log(1-0.9)=\log(0.1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}(\neg{car},t,w)=\log(1-0.9)=\log(0.1)</annotation></semantics></math> is low.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The LogSTOP for <math id="A1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{1}\land\varphi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{1}\land\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> at timestep <math id="A1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is high only when the scores for both <math id="A1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are high (line <a href="#alg1.l12" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 LogSTOP: An Algorithm for Computing STOPs ‣ LogSTOP: Temporal Scores over Prediction Sequences for Matching and Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). For example, given high scores <math id="A1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}({car},t,w)=\log(0.9)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}({car},t,w)=\log(0.9)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}({pedestrian},t,w)=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}({pedestrian},t,w)=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>, the score for "car and pedestrian" <math id="A1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}({car}\land{pedestrian},t,w)=\log(0.9)+\log(0.9)=\log(0.81)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.81</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}({car}\land{pedestrian},t,w)=\log(0.9)+\log(0.9)=\log(0.81)</annotation></semantics></math>. However, if either of the scores are low, e.g., if <math id="A1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}({pedestrian},t,w)=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}({pedestrian},t,w)=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, the score drops significantly to <math id="A1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}({car}\land{pedestrian},t,w)=\log(0.9)+\log(0.1)=\log(0.09)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.09</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}({car}\land{pedestrian},t,w)=\log(0.9)+\log(0.1)=\log(0.09)</annotation></semantics></math>. Inspired by DeMorgan’s law, the LogSTOP for <math id="A1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{1}\lor\varphi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{1}\lor\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is simply the score for equivalent property <math id="A1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neg(\neg\varphi_{1}\land\neg\varphi_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neg(\neg\varphi_{1}\land\neg\varphi_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> (line <a href="#alg1.l14" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 LogSTOP: An Algorithm for Computing STOPs ‣ LogSTOP: Temporal Scores over Prediction Sequences for Matching and Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>). This is intuitively only low when both the scores are low.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that these operators are defined not only over local properties <math id="A1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> as in the examples above, but over any temporal property <math id="A1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi,\varphi_{1},\varphi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi,\varphi_{1},\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, for temporal property <math id="A1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> = "(car or truck) and (not pedestrian)", the score is high only if it is high for both <math id="A1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> = "(car or truck)" and <math id="A1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> = "(not pedestrian)". The scores for <math id="A1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{1},\varphi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{1},\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> can be recursively computed.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Handling of temporal operators (<math id="A1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcirc,\square,\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">○</mo><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" lspace="0em">𝒰</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcirc,\square,\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span> 
As discussed above, the property Next <math id="A1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcirc\,\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.392em">○</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcirc\,\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>) evaluates whether <math id="A1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is expressed starting at the next block <math id="A1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+w</annotation></semantics></math> (line <a href="#alg1.l16" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 LogSTOP: An Algorithm for Computing STOPs ‣ LogSTOP: Temporal Scores over Prediction Sequences for Matching and Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>). When <math id="A1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=1</annotation></semantics></math>, this represents the standard Next operator.
The property Always <math id="A1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square\,\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square\,\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>) is interpreted here as a "temporal and" operator over the sequence (line <a href="#alg1.l18" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 LogSTOP: An Algorithm for Computing STOPs ‣ LogSTOP: Temporal Scores over Prediction Sequences for Matching and Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>). Hence, <math id="A1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square\,\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square\,\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> can be equivalently written as <math id="A1.p6.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\varphi\land\bigcirc\,\square\,\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo rspace="0em">∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.392em">○</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\land\bigcirc\,\square\,\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>: the property <math id="A1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is expressed by <math id="A1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and always after by <math id="A1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x_{t+w},\ldots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x_{t+w},\ldots]</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcirc\,{\square\,\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.392em">○</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcirc\,{\square\,\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math>). Similar to the logical <math id="A1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\land" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∧</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\land</annotation></semantics></math> operator, the score is high only if it is high for all timesteps of the sequence. The computation in <math id="A1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>log</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log</annotation></semantics></math> space is beneficial to prevent any underflow here with fixed precision.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The property <math id="A1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> Until <math id="A1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi=\varphi_{1}\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}\varphi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">𝒰</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi=\varphi_{1}\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) can be equivalently written as <math id="A1.p7.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\varphi=\varphi_{2}\lor(\neg\varphi_{2}\land\varphi_{1}\land\bigcirc\,(\varphi_{1}\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}\varphi_{2}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.392em">○</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">𝒰</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi=\varphi_{2}\lor(\neg\varphi_{2}\land\varphi_{1}\land\bigcirc\,(\varphi_{1}\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}\varphi_{2}))</annotation></semantics></math> (line <a href="#alg1.l20" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 LogSTOP: An Algorithm for Computing STOPs ‣ LogSTOP: Temporal Scores over Prediction Sequences for Matching and Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>). This informally translates to evaluating whether either (1) <math id="A1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is expressed by <math id="A1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, or (2) <math id="A1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is expressed instead and <math id="A1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi=\varphi_{1}\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}\varphi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">𝒰</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi=\varphi_{1}\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is expressed by <math id="A1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x_{t+w},\ldots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x_{t+w},\ldots]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>More Details on Quantitative Semantics for Temporal Logic</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now present more details on the standard quantitative semantics for Signal Temporal Logic (STL) discussed in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 LogSTOP: An Algorithm for Computing STOPs ‣ LogSTOP: Temporal Scores over Prediction Sequences for Matching and Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. A formula in Signal Temporal Logic can be written as:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\varphi:=\top\mid\mu\mid\neg\varphi\mid\varphi_{1}\land\varphi_{2}\mid\varphi_{1}\mathbin{\mathcal{U}_{I}}\varphi_{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊤</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>φ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><mi>φ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>φ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mi>φ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:=\top\mid\mu\mid\neg\varphi\mid\varphi_{1}\land\varphi_{2}\mid\varphi_{1}\mathbin{\mathcal{U}_{I}}\varphi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=f(s(t))\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=f(s(t))\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> is a Lipschitz continuous function over the signal <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=[t_{1},t_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=[t_{1},t_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> is a time interval, <math id="A2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{2}\geq t_{1}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{2}\geq t_{1}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. The operators <span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Eventually</span> and <span id="A2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Always</span> can be defined as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Diamond_{I}\varphi:=\top\mathbin{\mathcal{U}_{I}}\varphi" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>I</mi></msub><mi>φ</mi><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊤</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Diamond_{I}\varphi:=\top\mathbin{\mathcal{U}_{I}}\varphi</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square_{I}\varphi:=\neg\Diamond_{I}\neg\varphi" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square_{I}\varphi:=\neg\Diamond_{I}\neg\varphi</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of the STOP problem, <math id="A2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(t)=\hat{y}(X,\cdot,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(t)=\hat{y}(X,\cdot,t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c}(s(t))=\hat{y}(X,c,t)-\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c}(s(t))=\hat{y}(X,c,t)-\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. The query "car until pedestrian" can be written as <math id="A2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{car}(s(t))\mathbin{\mathcal{U}_{I}}\mu_{pedestrian}(s(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{car}(s(t))\mathbin{\mathcal{U}_{I}}\mu_{pedestrian}(s(t))</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{car}(s(t))=\hat{y}(X,{car},t)-\tau\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{car}(s(t))=\hat{y}(X,{car},t)-\tau\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{pedestrian}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{pedestrian}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined similarly).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The STL quantitative semantics, also called <span id="A2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">robustness</span> <math id="A2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, is defined as follows to indicate how much a signal satisfies or violates the formula:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\top,s,t):=\rho_{\top}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊤</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\top,s,t):=\rho_{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\mu,s,t):=f(s(t))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\mu,s,t):=f(s(t))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\neg\varphi,s,t):=-\rho(\varphi,s,t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\neg\varphi,s,t):=-\rho(\varphi,s,t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\varphi_{1}\land\varphi_{2},s,t):=\min(\rho(\varphi_{1},s,t),\rho(\varphi_{2},s,t))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\varphi_{1}\land\varphi_{2},s,t):=\min(\rho(\varphi_{1},s,t),\rho(\varphi_{2},s,t))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\varphi_{1}\lor\varphi_{2},s,t):=\max(\rho(\varphi_{1},s,t),\rho(\varphi_{2},s,t))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\varphi_{1}\lor\varphi_{2},s,t):=\max(\rho(\varphi_{1},s,t),\rho(\varphi_{2},s,t))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\varphi_{1}\mathbin{\mathcal{U}_{I}}\varphi_{2},s,t):=\sup_{t^{\prime}\in\,t+I}(\min\{\rho(\varphi_{2},s,t^{\prime}),\inf_{t^{\prime\prime}\in\,[t,t^{\prime}]}\rho(\varphi_{1},s,t^{\prime\prime})\})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.448em">∈</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.448em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\varphi_{1}\mathbin{\mathcal{U}_{I}}\varphi_{2},s,t):=\sup_{t^{\prime}\in\,t+I}(\min\{\rho(\varphi_{2},s,t^{\prime}),\inf_{t^{\prime\prime}\in\,[t,t^{\prime}]}\rho(\varphi_{1},s,t^{\prime\prime})\})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\square_{I}\,\varphi,s,t):=\inf_{t^{\prime}\in\,[t+I]}\rho(\varphi,s,t^{\prime})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.448em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\square_{I}\,\varphi,s,t):=\inf_{t^{\prime}\in\,[t+I]}\rho(\varphi,s,t^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\Diamond_{I}\,\varphi,s,t):=\sup_{t^{\prime}\in\,[t+I]}\rho(\varphi,s,t^{\prime})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.448em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\Diamond_{I}\,\varphi,s,t):=\sup_{t^{\prime}\in\,[t+I]}\rho(\varphi,s,t^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">where, <math id="A2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum robustness, i.e., <math id="A2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\top}=b-\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\top}=b-\tau</annotation></semantics></math> for the CSTOP problem where <math id="A2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=\max(\hat{y}(\cdot,\cdot,\cdot))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=\max(\hat{y}(\cdot,\cdot,\cdot))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We argue that LogSTOP offers advantages over such semantics in the context of the STOP problem.
This is primarily because the traditional robustness measure is defined using <math id="A2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>max</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>min</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min</annotation></semantics></math> functions over temporal and logical formulae.
The measure, hence, only reflects the most violating or most satisfying timestep in the sequence. For example, consider assigning confidence scores to the property "Always car" in two different scenarios:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{1}({car},t\in[0,2])=[\log(0.9),\log(0.9),\log(0.1)]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{1}({car},t\in[0,2])=[\log(0.9),\log(0.9),\log(0.1)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{2}({car},t\in[0,2])=[\log(0.1),\log(0.1),\log(0.1)]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{2}({car},t\in[0,2])=[\log(0.1),\log(0.1),\log(0.1)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p9.2" class="ltx_p">Ideally, the confidence score for "Always car" should follow the order:
<math id="A2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{1}(\square{car},\cdot)&gt;\hat{y}_{2}(\square{car},\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{1}(\square{car},\cdot)&gt;\hat{y}_{2}(\square{car},\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The standard STL semantics, however, would assign the same robustness to both sequences for any <math id="A2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0.1</annotation></semantics></math> since the most violating score is <math id="A2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(0.1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(0.1)</annotation></semantics></math> in either case. This makes the robustness measure unsuitable for downstream applications that require such ordering: for example, ranking / search.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">An example with Boolean operators. </span> For example, consider assigning confidence scores to the property "car and pedestrian" at <math id="A2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> in two different scenarios:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{1}({car},t=0)=\log(0.9),\hat{y}_{1}({pedestrian},t=0)=\log(0.6)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{1}({car},t=0)=\log(0.9),\hat{y}_{1}({pedestrian},t=0)=\log(0.6)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{2}({car},t=0)=\log(0.6),\hat{y}_{2}({pedestrian},t=0)=\log(0.6)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{2}({car},t=0)=\log(0.6),\hat{y}_{2}({pedestrian},t=0)=\log(0.6)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p10.2" class="ltx_p">Ideally, the confidence scores for "car and pedestrian" should follow the order:
<math id="A2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{1}({car}\land{pedestrian},t=0)&gt;\hat{y}_{2}({car}\land{pedestrian},t=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{1}({car}\land{pedestrian},t=0)&gt;\hat{y}_{2}({car}\land{pedestrian},t=0)</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="A2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{1}({car},t=0)&gt;\hat{y}_{2}({car},t=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{1}({car},t=0)&gt;\hat{y}_{2}({car},t=0)</annotation></semantics></math>. The robustness for both the cases is the same, i.e., <math id="A2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(0.6)-\log(0.5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(0.6)-\log(0.5)</annotation></semantics></math>, because of the <math id="A2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>min</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min</annotation></semantics></math> semantics for the Boolean <span id="A2.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span> operator. The LogSTOP for the two cases are <math id="A2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(0.9)+\log(0.6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(0.9)+\log(0.6)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(0.6)+\log(0.6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(0.6)+\log(0.6)</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, which reflect the expected order.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">An example with the Until operator. </span> Consider assigning confidence scores to the property "car Until pedestrian" in two different scenarios:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{1}({car},t\in[0,2])=[\log(0.6),\log(0.6),\log(0.6)]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{1}({car},t\in[0,2])=[\log(0.6),\log(0.6),\log(0.6)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{1}({pedestrian},t\in[0,2])=[\log(0.4),\log(0.4),\log(0.9)]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{1}({pedestrian},t\in[0,2])=[\log(0.4),\log(0.4),\log(0.9)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p11.2" class="ltx_p">and,</p>
<table id="A2.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{2}({car},t\in[0,2])=[\log(0.6),\log(0.6),\log(0.6)]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{2}({car},t\in[0,2])=[\log(0.6),\log(0.6),\log(0.6)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{2}({pedestrian},t\in[0,2])=[\log(0.4),\log(0.4),\log(0.6)]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{2}({pedestrian},t\in[0,2])=[\log(0.4),\log(0.4),\log(0.6)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the only difference between the two scenarios is the score for "pedestrian" at <math id="A2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=2</annotation></semantics></math> (a high score of <math id="A2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.9</annotation></semantics></math> for the first scenario and a lower score of <math id="A2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.6</annotation></semantics></math> for the second scenario) . The robustness for the two cases is the same because of the <math id="A2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>min</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min</annotation></semantics></math> semantics within the <span id="A2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Until</span> operator. LogSTOP assigns a higher score for the first scenario because of the difference at <math id="A2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>More Details on Datasets</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments ‣ LogSTOP: Temporal Scores over Prediction Sequences for Matching and Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we briefly discuss the QMTP and TP2VR benchmarks for evaluation.
For
constructing these benchmarks
, we use
three existing datasets with frame/segment-level annotations for local properties:
The RealTLV dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>
consists of videos from NuScenes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and Waymo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> driving datasets with frame-level annotations for 6 object classes.
(for example, "car", "truck", etc).
The IEMOCAP dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> provides speech segments from conversations
between two speakers
and each segment is labeled with one of the 4 major emotions expressed by the speaker.
(for example, "happy", "sad", etc.).
The AVA dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> consists of frame-level action annotations for 80 actions in 15-min clips from YouTube. We only consider the validation subset of this dataset and sample 5 frames per second.
These datasets can be used for evaluating
temporal properties over objects in videos
("car until pedestrian", for example)
, emotions in speech
("always happy")
, and actions in videos
("a person sits until they stand up")
respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The QMTP dataset.</span>
For any temporal property template (for example, "p1 Until p2") and samples from these datasets, we identify matching and non-matching sequences of desired length as follows: for every sample, we first identify candidates for local properties in the template (p1, p2, etc.) as the set of all ground-truth objects / emotions in the sequence. We then use the standard LTL semantics over the frame/segment-level ground-truth labels to collect matching and non-matching subsequences of the desired length. This creates a TP-query matching dataset for an arbitrary set of temporal properties as long as these properties are sufficiently expressed by sequences from the underlying dataset. Moreover, this pipeline is agnostic to the choice of the dataset since it only requires sequences of ground-truth labels for local properties. We use this pipeline to create
the <span id="A3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">QMTP-video dataset</span> with <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7468" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7468</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7468</annotation></semantics></math> samples (<math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3750</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3750</annotation></semantics></math> matching and <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3718" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3718</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3718</annotation></semantics></math> non-matching) with video sequences of lengths <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{10,20,30,40,50\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{10,20,30,40,50\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each target length, this dataset contains approximately <math id="A3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> samples corresponding to each of the <math id="A3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> property templates. Similarly, we create the <span id="A3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">QMTP-speech dataset</span> with <math id="A3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3300</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3300</annotation></semantics></math> samples, including speech sequences of lengths in ranges <math id="A3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{5-10,10-20,20-30\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{5-10,10-20,20-30\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The QMTP-speech dataset only contains samples from <math id="A3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11/15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11/15</annotation></semantics></math> property templates. This is because there are no sequences matching <math id="A3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> properties "Always p1 and Eventually p2", "Always (p1 and p2)", "(p1 and p2) Until p3", and "(p1 and p2) Until Eventually p3" since two emotions cannot be expressed at the same time.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The TP2VR dataset.</span>
We restrict queries to a maximum of 5 per temporal property template. For TP2VR objects, we aim to find <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25-50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25-50</annotation></semantics></math> frame-long subsequences satisfying a temporal property; we only include a temporal property if less than <math id="A3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="250" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>250</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">250</annotation></semantics></math> videos are retrieved using the standard LTL semantics with the ground truth labels.
With all possible combinations of objects, this gives us a total of <math id="A3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>42</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42</annotation></semantics></math> queries, with an average of <math id="A3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="163" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>163</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">163</annotation></semantics></math> videos relevant to a query.
Similarly, for TP2VR-actions, we aim to find 10-second long subsequences (<math id="A3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> frames) satisfying a temporal property; we only include a temporal property if less than <math id="A3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> videos are retrieved using the standard LTL semantics with the ground truth labels. With all possible combinations of actions, this gives us a total of <math id="A3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>70</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70</annotation></semantics></math> queries, with an average of <math id="A3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>21</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21</annotation></semantics></math> videos relevant to a query.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>More details on Query Matching Methods</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LogSTOP with simple trained neural predictors:</span> We use YOLOv8 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, Grounding DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, and OWLv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, to get confidence scores for object detection in video frames, and HuBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> for speech emotion recognition in audio segments. Since the scores are in the <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> range, we normalize them in the <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-\infty,0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-\infty,0]</annotation></semantics></math> range using the <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>log</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log</annotation></semantics></math> operation, as required by LogSTOP.
A video matches query <math id="A4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> if LogSTOP <math id="A4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}(\varphi,0,w)&gt;\tau(\varphi,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}(\varphi,0,w)&gt;\tau(\varphi,0)</annotation></semantics></math> (and vice versa for non-matching examples). The estimates are evaluated against ground-truth labels <math id="A4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y(\varphi,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y(\varphi,0)</annotation></semantics></math>. The window <math id="A4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is selected as follows: <math id="A4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=2</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T&lt;20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T&lt;20</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=5</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Large Vision Language Models (LVLMs)</span>. We evaluate two popular LVLMs on query matching for videos: <span id="A4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Video-LLava-7B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> and <span id="A4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LongVA-7B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>.
For the "always car" example, we provide the models with the video sequence and a text prompt "Is a car detected in all frames of this video?". The response is considered correct if the model responds with "Yes" or "No" for matching and non-matching samples respectively. <span id="A4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Video-LLava-7B</span> supports a context window of <math id="A4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4096</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4096</annotation></semantics></math> tokens while <span id="A4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LongVA-7B</span> can handle up to 2000 frames. We set the maximum tokens to generate to <math id="A4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>60</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1024</annotation></semantics></math> respectively and use a temperature of 0.1 and standard values for the other parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Large Audio Language Models (LALMs)</span>. Similarly, we evaluate two popular LALMs on query matching for speech: <span id="A4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen-Audio-Chat</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> and <span id="A4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2-Audio-7B-Instruct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
For the "eventually happy" example, we provide the models with the audio sequence and a text prompt "Does the speaker sound happy at some time in this audio clip?". We set the sampling rate to <math id="A4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16000</annotation></semantics></math> and generate a maximum of <math id="A4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math> new tokens, with standard values for other parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NSVS-TL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</span> Proposed for event detection in videos, NSVS-TL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> uses the PCTL-based model checker STORM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> to identify video frame subsequences where a certain event is detected. NSVS-TL reports state-of-the-art performance on detecting temporal events in videos, surpassing large language models such as GPT-4.
For our task, we specify the target query in PCTL ("always car" is <math id="A4.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P&gt;0.5[G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P&gt;0.5[G</annotation></semantics></math> "<math id="A4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{car}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{car}</annotation></semantics></math>" <math id="A4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">]</annotation></semantics></math>) and the response is considered correct if NSVS-TL returns / does not return the entire video sequence as output for a matching / non-matching query respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We do not evaluate the method from  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. [44]</a></cite> since the implementation is not publicly available and LTL model checking with STORM is not well-documented.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>More details on the Ranked Retrieval methods</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LogSTOP.</span>
We use LogSTOP with Grounding DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> for TP2VR-objects and with SlowR50 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> for TP2VR-actions respectively.
We repurpose the script from <span id="A5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tutorials/video_detection_example</span> at <span id="A5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/facebookresearch/pytorchvideo/</span>
to run SlowR50 on videos from the TP2VR-actions dataset.
We use a smoothing window <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=5</annotation></semantics></math> for all retrieval experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">mPLUG.</span>
We use the implementation from <span id="A5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/alibaba/AliceMind</span>. We repurpose the <span id="A5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mPLUG/retrieval_vid_mplug.py</span> script to run <span id="A5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mPLUG_large_v2</span> on videos and queries from the TP2VR datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CaptionSim.</span>
Inspired by ELIOT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, we also include embedding similarity between video captions and text queries as a baseline.
We refer to this as <span id="A5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CaptionSim</span> in the discussion, and use
<span id="A5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaVA-NeXT-Video-7B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> for generating video captions and <span id="A5.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SentenceBERT/all-MiniLM-L6-v2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> for embedding captions and queries.
Due to the limited context window of <span id="A5.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaVA-NeXT-Video-7B</span>, we divide videos in sections of 50 frames and generate captions for each before concatenating them together. We use the following prompt to generate the captions for the first 50 frames: "Describe this video in detail, listing objects in each frame. Keep the descriptions concise." for TP2VR-objects.
For any next sections, we use the prompt "Continue describing the video, listing objects in each frame. You are now at frame i, you have already described the previous i frames."
For TP2VR-actions, we use the prompt "Describe this video in detail, listing actions and objects in each frame. Keep the descriptions concise." We set the <span id="A5.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">max_new_tokens</span> to 1024.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We use <span id="A5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CaptionSim</span> because the implementation of ELIOT is not publicly available. Our local implementation of ELIOT did not report good results (the video captions generated by  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> did not include mentions of objects or actions).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Queries and Prompts</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We choose <math id="A6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> temporal property templates for the experiments in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments ‣ LogSTOP: Temporal Scores over Prediction Sequences for Matching and Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The LogSTOP queries for these templates are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="A6.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Eventually p1: <math id="A6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Diamond\,p1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Diamond\,p1</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Always p1: <math id="A6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square\,p1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square\,p1</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">p1 Until p2: <math id="A6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p1\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}p2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">𝒰</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p1\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}p2</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Always p1 and Eventually p2: <math id="A6.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square\,p1\land\Diamond\,p2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square\,p1\land\Diamond\,p2</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Always p1 or Eventually p2: <math id="A6.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square\,p1\lor\Diamond\,p2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square\,p1\lor\Diamond\,p2</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">6.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(Not p1) Until p2: <math id="A6.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neg p1\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}p2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">𝒰</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neg p1\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}p2</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">7.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">p1 Until (Not p2): <math id="A6.I1.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p1\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}\neg p2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mo class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">𝒰</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p1\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}\neg p2</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">8.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Always (p1 and p2): <math id="A6.I1.i8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square(p1\land p2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square(p1\land p2)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">9.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(p1 and p2) Until p3: <math id="A6.I1.i9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p1\land p2)\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}p3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">𝒰</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p1\land p2)\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}p3</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i10" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">10.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">p1 Until Always p2: <math id="A6.I1.i10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p1\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}\square\,p2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">𝒰</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p1\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}\square\,p2</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i11" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">11.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i11.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Eventually Always p1: <math id="A6.I1.i11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Diamond\,\square\,p1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Diamond\,\square\,p1</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i12" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">12.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i12.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Always Eventually p1: <math id="A6.I1.i12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square\,\Diamond\,p1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square\,\Diamond\,p1</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i13" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">13.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i13.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(Not p1) Until Eventually p2: <math id="A6.I1.i13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neg p1\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}\Diamond\,p2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">𝒰</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neg p1\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}\Diamond\,p2</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i14" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">14.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i14.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(Not p1) Until Always p2: <math id="A6.I1.i14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neg p1\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}\square\,p2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">𝒰</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neg p1\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}\square\,p2</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i15" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">15.</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(p1 and p2) Until Eventually p3: <math id="A6.I1.i15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p1\land p2)\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}\Diamond\,p3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">𝒰</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">◇</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p1\land p2)\mathbin{\mathcal{U}}\Diamond\,p3</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">NSVS-TL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> uses the model checker STORM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> to verify if a given sequence satisfies a temporal property, where the temporal properties are represented in Probabilistic Computation Tree Logic (PCTL). In PCTL, the <math id="A6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> operators represent the <span id="A6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Eventually</span>, <span id="A6.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Always</span> and <span id="A6.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Until</span> operators respectively. The <math id="A6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\&amp;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&amp;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\&amp;</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mid" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∣</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mid</annotation></semantics></math> operators represent the Boolean <span id="A6.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">negation</span>, <span id="A6.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span>, and <span id="A6.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">or</span> operators respectively. The operator <math id="A6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is used to indicate the ranges of probability of a given property being satisfied: for example, <math id="A6.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P&gt;0.5[F\,\varphi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P&gt;0.5[F\,\varphi]</annotation></semantics></math> translates to "the probability of <math id="A6.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> eventually being satisfied is more than <math id="A6.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math>".</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The PCTL queries for the 15 temporal property templates are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="A6.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A6.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A6.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Eventually p1: <math id="A6.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P&gt;0.5\,[F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P&gt;0.5\,[F</annotation></semantics></math> "<math id="A6.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p1}</annotation></semantics></math>" <math id="A6.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A6.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Always p1: <math id="A6.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P&gt;0.5\,[G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P&gt;0.5\,[G</annotation></semantics></math> "<math id="A6.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p1}</annotation></semantics></math>" <math id="A6.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A6.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">p1 Until p2: <math id="A6.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P&gt;0.5\,[" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P&gt;0.5\,[</annotation></semantics></math> "<math id="A6.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p1}</annotation></semantics></math>" <math id="A6.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> "<math id="A6.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p2}</annotation></semantics></math>" <math id="A6.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A6.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Always p1 and Eventually p2: <math id="A6.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P&gt;0.5\,[\,G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P&gt;0.5\,[\,G</annotation></semantics></math> "<math id="A6.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p1}</annotation></semantics></math>" <math id="A6.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\&amp;\,F\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">&amp;</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\&amp;\,F\,</annotation></semantics></math> "<math id="A6.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p2}</annotation></semantics></math>" <math id="A6.I2.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="A6.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Always p1 or Eventually p2: <math id="A6.I2.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P&gt;0.5\,[\,G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P&gt;0.5\,[\,G</annotation></semantics></math> "<math id="A6.I2.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p1}</annotation></semantics></math>" <math id="A6.I2.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mid\,F\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∣</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mid\,F\,</annotation></semantics></math> "<math id="A6.I2.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p2}</annotation></semantics></math>" <math id="A6.I2.i5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I2.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">6.</span> 
<div id="A6.I2.i6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I2.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(Not p1) Until p2: <math id="A6.I2.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P&gt;0.5\,[\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">∼</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P&gt;0.5\,[\sim</annotation></semantics></math> "<math id="A6.I2.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p1}</annotation></semantics></math>" <math id="A6.I2.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> "<math id="A6.I2.i6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p2}</annotation></semantics></math>" <math id="A6.I2.i6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I2.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">7.</span> 
<div id="A6.I2.i7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I2.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">p1 Until (Not p2): <math id="A6.I2.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P&gt;0.5\,[" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P&gt;0.5\,[</annotation></semantics></math> "<math id="A6.I2.i7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p1}</annotation></semantics></math>" <math id="A6.I2.i7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∼</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\sim</annotation></semantics></math> "<math id="A6.I2.i7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p2}</annotation></semantics></math>" <math id="A6.I2.i7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I2.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">8.</span> 
<div id="A6.I2.i8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
